delivery:index:Agent.description:
Declares a task for a Custom Agent of the organization that a run executes in a stage. The agent works on the prompt, in the repository of source when one is set, and returns a typed result that the result schema describes.

The prompt can use the outputs of other members with apply. The pass that declares the task is then a later pass of the run.

A resource that is not a Delivery resource waits for the task when it uses an output of the task.

## Example Usage

```typescript
import * as delivery from "@pulumi/delivery";

const fix = new delivery.Agent("fix", {
    stage: implement,
    agent: "implementer",
    source: code,
    prompt: pulumi.interpolate`Implement ${issue.data.apply(data => data["identifier"])}: ${issue.data.apply(data => data["title"])}`,
    resultSchema: { type: "object", properties: { pullRequest: { type: "integer" } }, required: ["pullRequest"] },
});
export const pullRequest = fix.result.apply(result => result["pullRequest"]);
```

delivery:index:Artifact.description:
Publishes a value for the pipelines that read this stage with a PipelineSource. The artifact is named like the resource. It succeeds once its stage is admitted and its value is known.

## Example Usage

```typescript
import * as delivery from "@pulumi/delivery";

new delivery.Artifact("clusterEndpoint", {
    stage: prod,
    value: cluster.endpoint,
});
```

delivery:index:GitHubSource.description:
Declares a GitHub repository and branch. Members check out this source. The source locks its commit when the run first creates it, and every stage of the run gets that commit. The commit output is that commit. It is unknown during registration and preview.

A source does not start runs unless you set trigger. Set trigger to true to start a run on every push to the branch, or to { paths } to start one only when a push changes a matching file.

## Example Usage

```typescript
import * as delivery from "@pulumi/delivery";

const code = new delivery.GitHubSource("code", {
    repository: "acme/shop",
    branch: "main",
    trigger: { paths: ["src/**", "delivery/**"] },
});
```

delivery:index:LinearIssueSource.description:
Declares a WebhookSource for the issue webhooks of Linear, with a fixed map and a when built from the filters. Point a Linear webhook for issues at the url output. Linear signs each post with HMAC-SHA256 over the raw body in the Linear-Signature header.

A post starts a run when it is about an issue that was created or updated, every label in labels is on the issue, the state of the issue is one of states, and (with ignoreBots) a user made the change. The subject is the id of the issue, the version is when the issue changed, and the data holds identifier, title, description, url, state, labels, team and action.

## Example Usage

```typescript
import * as delivery from "@pulumi/delivery";

const issues = new delivery.LinearIssueSource("issues", {
    signingSecret: config.requireSecret("linearSigningSecret"),
    labels: ["neon"],
    states: ["Todo"],
});
export const issuesUrl = issues.url;
```

delivery:index:Pipeline.description:
Declares the pipeline: the source and directory of the pipeline program itself, and the release number of the run. A program has one Pipeline.

Run pulumi up on the pipeline stack to register it. Registration ends with exit code 10, because the members wait for a run.

## Example Usage

```typescript
import * as delivery from "@pulumi/delivery";

const code = new delivery.GitHubSource("code", {
    repository: "acme/shop",
    branch: "main",
    trigger: true,
});
const pipeline = new delivery.Pipeline("shop", {
    program: { source: code, directory: "delivery" },
});
export const release = pipeline.releaseNumber;
```

delivery:index:PipelineSource.description:
Reads the release that a stage of another pipeline in the same organization deployed. The outputs are unknown during registration and preview. If any artifact is secret, the whole map is secret.

A pipeline source is not a checkout source: a job cannot check it out.

A pipeline source does not start runs unless you set trigger: true. Then a new release of the upstream stage starts a run of this pipeline.

## Example Usage

```typescript
import * as delivery from "@pulumi/delivery";

const platform = new delivery.PipelineSource("platform", {
    pipeline: "acme/platform-delivery/main",
    stage: "prod",
    trigger: true,
});
const endpoint = platform.artifacts.apply(a => a["clusterEndpoint"]);
```

delivery:index:Stacks.description:
Updates one or more existing stacks in a stage. Each entry of stacks is one update of a target stack. Delivery checks out the source of the entry at the commit of the run and runs pulumi up on the target stack.

Create each target stack before the first run. A target stack must be an ordinary stack in the organization of the pipeline.

The entries of a group run together and can read each other's new outputs through stack references. The group is one unit for dependencies and retries: a resource that uses an output of one entry waits for the whole group, and a retry runs every entry again if one entry failed. Each entry uses one slot of the concurrent job limit of the organization. A group with more entries than the limit can block itself, and Delivery fails it after 2 hours without progress.

The outputs of the group are secret as a whole if the output of one entry is secret.

## Example Usage

```typescript
import * as delivery from "@pulumi/delivery";

new delivery.Stacks("dev", {
    stage: dev,
    stacks: {
        api: { stack: "acme/shop-infra/dev", source: code, directory: "infra", config: { environment: "dev" } },
    },
});
```

delivery:index:Stage.description:
Declares a stage of the pipeline. The from stages define promotion edges, and when adds an optional rule such as approval. A stage with several upstream stages waits for the same release to converge in all of them.

## Example Usage

```typescript
import * as delivery from "@pulumi/delivery";

const dev = new delivery.Stage("dev", { pipeline });
new delivery.Stage("prod", {
    pipeline,
    from: [dev],
    when: delivery.approval({ approvers: ["your-username"] }),
});
```

delivery:index:WebhookSource.description:
Declares a source that an external system posts to. The url output is where it posts. The service verifies each post with signing, checks it against when, and maps it with map to the subject, version and data of the run that the post starts. Every stage of the run sees that value.

The subject, version and data outputs are what the map expression yielded for the post that started the run. They are unknown during registration and preview.

A webhook source is not a checkout source: a job cannot check it out.

## Example Usage

```typescript
import * as delivery from "@pulumi/delivery";

const orders = new delivery.WebhookSource("orders", {
    signing: { kind: "hmac-sha256", secret: config.requireSecret("ordersSecret") },
    when: "event == 'order.created'",
    map: "{subject: order.id, version: order.updatedAt, data: order}",
});
export const ordersUrl = orders.url;
```

delivery:index:allOf.description:
Builds an all rule for a Stage's when: passes once every child rule passes.

## Example Usage

```typescript
import * as delivery from "@pulumi/delivery";

const prod = new delivery.Stage("prod", {
    pipeline,
    from: [dev],
    when: delivery.allOfOutput({
        rules: [delivery.approvalOutput({ approvers: ["your-username"] })],
    }),
});
```

delivery:index:anyOf.description:
Builds an any rule for a Stage's when: passes once one child rule passes.

## Example Usage

```typescript
import * as delivery from "@pulumi/delivery";

const prod = new delivery.Stage("prod", {
    pipeline,
    from: [dev],
    when: delivery.anyOfOutput({
        rules: [delivery.approvalOutput({ approvers: ["your-username"] }), delivery.manualOutput()],
    }),
});
```

delivery:index:approval.description:
Builds an approval rule for a Stage's when. The gate waits until the required number of eligible people approve. Omitted or zero count means one approval. The person who started the run cannot approve it. Without named approvers, anyone with the organization update permission can approve. Approving needs the Approvals feature.

## Example Usage

```typescript
import * as delivery from "@pulumi/delivery";

const prod = new delivery.Stage("prod", {
    pipeline,
    from: [dev],
    when: delivery.approval({ approvers: ["your-username"] }),
});
```

delivery:index:decision.description:
Builds a decision rule for a Stage's when. The gate asks a Decision Agent of the organization, with the inputs as the fields of its prompt, and passes when the agent decides to continue. The inputs are usually built from the outputs of members of earlier stages, so the pass that declares the rule can be a later pass of the run.

## Example Usage

```typescript
import * as delivery from "@pulumi/delivery";

const merge = new delivery.Stage("merge", {
    pipeline,
    from: [implement],
    when: delivery.decisionOutput({
        agent: "reviewer",
        inputs: { pullRequest: fix.result.apply(result => result["pullRequest"]) },
    }),
});
```

delivery:index:http.description:
Builds an http rule for a Stage's when.

## Example Usage

```typescript
import * as delivery from "@pulumi/delivery";

const prod = new delivery.Stage("prod", {
    pipeline,
    from: [dev],
    when: delivery.http({ url: "https://example.com/check", successExpression: "status == 'ok'" }),
});
```

delivery:index:manual.description:
Builds a manual rule for a Stage's when. A manual gate waits until someone continues it, from the console or with a POST to the continue route of the gate. It works on every plan. Use approval() when you need named approvers and the Approvals feature.

## Example Usage

```typescript
import * as delivery from "@pulumi/delivery";

const prod = new delivery.Stage("prod", {
    pipeline,
    from: [dev],
    when: delivery.manual({ name: "dashboards", description: "Check the dashboards before you continue." }),
});
```
